  "worked_solution": "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=5{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}-10,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=5{y}^{4}+2{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2}-10\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={h}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(5{y}^{4}+2{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2}-10) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =20{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-16y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{20{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-16y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${h}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={h}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{h}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{20({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}+6({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}-16({h}^{-1}(x))} \\\\ {({h}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1}{20({h}^{-1}(x))^{3}+6({h}^{-1}(x))^{2}-16({h}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
